BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:<images/robolab/VERSION> is never published
(docs/PROTOCOL.md §2.4, D5). An evaluation host builds it locally, or takes it from the organization's own non-public
copy of the package (docs/PROTOCOL.md §7, item 1); the RoboLab tasks reference that tag and Harbor finds it in the local
Docker image store.

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the Isaac Lab 2.3.2 pip wheels (installed from
  pypi.nvidia.com). NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published.
  RoboLab itself (github.com/NVlabs/RoboLab v0.3.1, code and its ~6 GB LFS asset library) is Apache-2.0.

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and
    every container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim / isaaclab wheels on pypi.nvidia.com;
  - RoboLab's Apache-2.0 licence (its assets are baked in, as RoboLab's own docker/Dockerfile does).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Data
  None beyond the image: the RoboLab asset library is baked in, and since 0.1.2 so are the remote files its USD layers
  reference (below); nothing is mounted or downloaded at run time. The frozen task instances live in the task
  directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 5.1 supports
  (Isaac renders with RTX even headless; PhysX runs on the CPU). Checked on RTX 4090 (laptop and desktop) and RTX PRO
  6000. Build: ~40 GB of free disk, network access to github.com (+ its LFS), astral.sh, pypi.org, pypi.nvidia.com and
  NVIDIA's Omniverse content bucket (omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com and its s3-us-west-2
  spelling), about 45-60 min. Run: one Isaac simulator per 32 GB of host RAM (the standard sidecar and its replays run one at a time).

Build (from the repository root)
  docker build -f images/robolab/Dockerfile -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:$(cat images/robolab/VERSION) .
  (scripts/build_images.sh builds every images/<sim>/ with its own naming; until it names images eai-<sim>, use the
  command above.) The build context is the repository root: the Dockerfile copies images/robolab/uv.lock, which pins
  every Python package (torch 2.7.0+cu128, numpy 1.26.0, isaacsim 5.1.0.0, isaaclab 2.3.2.post1).

Check a build
  The frozen instances must rebuild to their recorded state hashes, and a fresh-process replay of a reference trajectory
  must end in its recorded hash. With a task directory at hand:
    docker run --rm --gpus all --shm-size 8g -v $PWD/tasks/robolab-banana-in-bowl-i00-privileged:/t \
      ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:$(cat images/robolab/VERSION) bash -c \
      "cd /tmp && python /t/environment/rcb_robolab.py run-oracle --task banana_in_bowl --instance /t/environment/instance.json \
         --code /t/solution/oracle_banana_in_bowl.py --out /tmp/t.npz && \
       python /t/environment/rcb_robolab.py replay --task banana_in_bowl --instance /t/environment/instance.json --traj /tmp/t.npz"
  expects instance_exact true (instance sha 7c211bc3fe6d80cd), success true, and the same final state_sha from both
  commands. Compare the two commands, not hosts: the final hash can differ between hosts (two values seen on ours,
  61afa384566be3a5 and 902e40deb3d42ed0, both successes). Or run `harbor run -p tasks/robolab-banana-in-bowl-i00-privileged -a oracle`.

On a CI machine (maintainers)
  A self-hosted runner with such a GPU builds the image with the command above when images/robolab/ or VERSION changes,
  runs the check, and keeps the image in its local Docker store (or in a registry the organization keeps private), never
  on a public registry. Hosts of the same organization may copy a built image among themselves
  (docker save ... | zstd, docker load); a new VERSION means a rebuild on every host.

Versions
  0.1.2 (2026-10-11): the asset library needs no network. RoboLab's USD layers reference NVIDIA's public Omniverse
  content bucket by URL (the MDL materials of the tables, the bins and the robot, their textures, a few props); Kit
  fetched them at scene build, and a container whose egress is the model APIs only rendered the table red. The last
  two instructions of the Dockerfile run mirror_remote_assets.py: 419 files (635 MB) copied to
  /opt/robolab/assets/_remote, each checked against remote_assets.sha256 (the build fails when the bucket serves other
  bytes, a file less or a file more; `--write` re-pins, after a look at the rendering), and the 15 layers that named
  them point at the copies. Python packages, RoboLab's code and the physics assets are those of 0.1.1.
  The organization's copy of 0.1.2 (digest sha256:3503e3b554f313f1e62e8f53c9f771c5743f3f67c07ea44d12ac72fc267b9ea6)
  was made as a delta, so that a host with 0.1.1 fetches one 651 MB layer: 0.1.1
  (sha256:1b3194b90fe3991303070a78d041126ff91717d0b5777b14b9ae5b6b9fc3b0d6) plus exactly those two instructions,
    printf 'FROM ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:0.1.1@sha256:1b3194b90fe3991303070a78d041126ff91717d0b5777b14b9ae5b6b9fc3b0d6\nCOPY mirror_remote_assets.py remote_assets.sha256 /opt/robolab/\nRUN python /opt/robolab/mirror_remote_assets.py /opt/robolab/assets /opt/robolab/assets/_remote --check /opt/robolab/remote_assets.sha256\n' \
      | docker build -f - -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:0.1.2 images/robolab
  A full build with the command above gives the same files in other layers. (The copy of mirror_remote_assets.py inside
  the published 0.1.2 is the script before three checks were added to it: a file is compared with the pinned list before
  it is written or opened, a URL must map inside the mirror, and a layer that still names a served remote file fails the
  build. Run in 0.1.1 against the same pinned list, the script as it is here gives the same 419 files and rewrites the
  same 150 references.) Check of either, without network: every
  mirrored file has its pinned hash, and no text layer of the library names the bucket by URL any more (the binary
  layers were checked with pxr when 0.1.2 was built: none of the 409 layers kept a remote reference):
    docker run --rm --network none ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-robolab:0.1.2 sh -c \
      'cd /opt/robolab/assets/_remote && sha256sum -c --quiet /opt/robolab/remote_assets.sha256 && ! grep -rIlE "https?:/+omniverse-content" /opt/robolab/assets --include=*.usda'
